      "sequence_position": 11,
      "problem": "Given the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],$ find its inverse  ${A}^{-1}$ by performing row operations.",
      "worked_solution": "Let's consider the augmented matrix $[A\\,|\\,{I}_{3}]$ where ${I}_{3}$ is the $3\\times 3$ identity. Then, we apply the following row operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,{I}_{3}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 4 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,-1 & 2 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,-1 & 2 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\,\\,-2 & 0 & \\,\\,-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & \\,\\,-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & \\,\\,-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  \\\\  & =[{I}_{3}\\,|\\,{A}^{-1}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
